00:11:10.738 --> 00:11:13.248
Thank you. So

157
00:11:14.378 --> 00:11:17.978
moving a bit further, Dr.

158
00:11:17.978 --> 00:11:23.408
Leijten, based on your research, what does it take for a system

159
00:11:23.818 --> 00:11:26.728
or critical infrastructure itself to be robust?

160
00:11:27.538 --> 00:11:32.288
Why is this important and how is it different from resilience?

161
00:11:34.298 --> 00:11:36.738
So that's a number of questions.

162
00:11:36.738 --> 00:11:43.048
So let me start with: a robust system is one that

163
00:11:43.538 --> 00:11:50.378
is actually already very stable before

164
00:11:50.378 --> 00:11:55.698
it's under threat. So a resilient system is one that bounces back if

165
00:11:55.698 --> 00:11:57.978
it receives a blow, it bounces back.

166
00:11:57.978 --> 00:12:03.568
But a robust system doesn't collapse in the first place.

167
00:12:04.098 --> 00:12:06.378
So a resilient system first receives a blow,

168
00:12:06.378 --> 00:12:12.768
collapses, gets up again, and then functions again.

169
00:12:13.578 --> 00:12:16.699
Which is good, but it would be better if it doesn't collapse in

170
00:12:16.699 --> 00:12:22.659
the first place. And so a robust system instead is one that is

171
00:12:22.659 --> 00:12:28.579
for example redundant. So it is not vulnerable for choosing only

172
00:12:28.579 --> 00:12:31.259
one option and then it turns out that the option doesn't

173
00:12:31.259 --> 00:12:33.689
work or that the option comes under threat.

174
00:12:34.099 --> 00:12:38.219
So you always have plan B.

175
00:12:38.219 --> 00:12:39.939
That's basically the idea.

176
00:12:39.939 --> 00:12:45.449
And the more heterogeneous the community that works with the system,

177
00:12:45.939 --> 00:12:47.929
the larger the chance that it is robust.

178
00:12:48.979 --> 00:12:53.329
Because you have more of those plan B kind of options.

179
00:12:54.659 --> 00:12:58.099
So how to build such a thing?

180
00:12:58.099 --> 00:13:04.409
Well, a good starting point I would say is the existing system.

181
00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:08.459
If you design a completely new system, you typically try to

182
00:13:08.459 --> 00:13:14.019
optimize. And here I would like to refer to the work of French

183
00:13:14.019 --> 00:13:16.849
biologist Olivier Hamant, who

184
00:13:17.819 --> 00:13:22.499
has recently published quite a lot about this topic, like the

185
00:13:22.499 --> 00:13:25.089
difference between resilience and

186
00:13:26.059 --> 00:13:32.619
robustness. Where resilience is no longer considered a very

187
00:13:32.619 --> 00:13:34.619
efficient way to... Well, it is very efficient,

188
00:13:34.619 --> 00:13:39.179
but efficiency is actually not the best way to build these systems.

189
00:13:39.179 --> 00:13:43.009
So we should not design for one optimal solution,

190
00:13:43.579 --> 00:13:48.179
we should design a system that can maybe later on move into a

191
00:13:48.179 --> 00:13:51.859
different scenario. So I have some colleagues who are working on

192
00:13:51.859 --> 00:13:52.889
what they call

193
00:13:54.579 --> 00:14:01.139
adaptive pathways. Because the future is inherently

194
00:14:01.139 --> 00:14:05.379
uncertain in this cutting-edge technology, we do not know exactly

195
00:14:05.379 --> 00:14:08.579
where we will be like 40, 50 years from now.

196
00:14:08.579 --> 00:14:10.459
What technology will look like?

197
00:14:10.459 --> 00:14:15.051
Like the technologies that we are using, be it AI or

198
00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:17.651
energy systems or whatever.

199
00:14:18.101 --> 00:14:21.291
So, betting on only one horse is very risky.

200
00:14:21.901 --> 00:14:26.901
Instead, you can develop a number of scenarios where you still

201
00:14:26.901 --> 00:14:29.581
don't know what the dot on the horizon is where you're moving

202
00:14:29.581 --> 00:14:35.821
towards, but you can identify perhaps a few junctions where you

203
00:14:35.821 --> 00:14:41.141
could change course. If this happens, then we need to move into the

204
00:14:41.141 --> 00:14:42.571
different scenario.

205
00:14:43.581 --> 00:14:46.451
And that is what I would call a robust system.

206
00:14:47.581 --> 00:14:48.651
If that makes sense.

207
00:14:49.781 --> 00:14:53.741
And could you give a more practical example of those

208
00:14:53.741 --> 00:14:58.741
systems, maybe in the Netherlands. Where do

209
00:14:58.741 --> 00:15:00.581
