210
00:15:00.581 --> 00:15:04.261
How are they deployed? In the private sector and public sector as

211
00:15:04.261 --> 00:15:06.541
well. We'll talk more about it later.

212
00:15:06.541 --> 00:15:11.621
Well, if you allow me, I have an interesting example not from the

213
00:15:11.621 --> 00:15:15.731
Netherlands, but from Houston, the United States.

214
00:15:16.461 --> 00:15:21.461
I visit Houston quite a lot, including one time with a group of

215
00:15:21.461 --> 00:15:26.381
students of mine, and Houston deals with high water problems.

216
00:15:26.381 --> 00:15:28.461
A problem that we have had in the Netherlands as well.

217
00:15:28.461 --> 00:15:30.171
So, that's where the connection is.

218
00:15:30.701 --> 00:15:34.061
And every now and then there is a hurricane,

219
00:15:34.061 --> 00:15:38.221
there's a lot of rainfall, and basically the whole city floods

220
00:15:38.221 --> 00:15:40.571
because it's all concrete and asphalt.

221
00:15:41.781 --> 00:15:46.221
Now there are two universities developing extremely expensive

222
00:15:46.221 --> 00:15:50.261
plans to build all kinds of dike systems, and I don't know what they

223
00:15:50.261 --> 00:15:53.611
intend to do. It will cost billions of dollars,

224
00:15:54.101 --> 00:15:56.421
and I guarantee you they will never get those,

225
00:15:56.421 --> 00:15:59.171
particularly not under the current federal government.

226
00:16:00.141 --> 00:16:04.091
So then, a bit later, we visited

227
00:16:05.061 --> 00:16:09.261
a place in downtown Houston where they had the acute problem

228
00:16:09.261 --> 00:16:12.291
that in case of any rainfall, the street would flood.

229
00:16:13.421 --> 00:16:16.181
And what they did was, you know, there was just

230
00:16:16.181 --> 00:16:19.062
the people from the city administration asking, what can we do about

231
00:16:19.062 --> 00:16:23.622
this? And what they did was, here and there, they broke up the

232
00:16:23.622 --> 00:16:26.212
pavement and built just a small basin.

233
00:16:27.142 --> 00:16:30.742
Because the issue was that the sewage system could not handle

234
00:16:30.742 --> 00:16:33.732
the kind of rainfall in a very short amount of time.

235
00:16:34.822 --> 00:16:39.382
So what they did, they built a basin with access to the sewage system

236
00:16:39.382 --> 00:16:43.062
but elevated, so that the rain would first have a chance to

237
00:16:43.062 --> 00:16:44.172
sink into the soil.

238
00:16:45.222 --> 00:16:48.782
And only then, when that overflows, does it flow into the sewage

239
00:16:48.782 --> 00:16:52.052
system, and then the capacity is suddenly sufficient.

240
00:16:52.982 --> 00:16:56.262
They planted a few bushes in there, so people thought that it

241
00:16:56.262 --> 00:16:58.702
actually was a beautification project.

242
00:16:58.702 --> 00:17:05.222
It wasn't. But the reason this solution worked, and a lot better

243
00:17:05.222 --> 00:17:09.182
than those billion-dollar plans that the universities had, was it

244
00:17:09.182 --> 00:17:10.892
was very small, it was scalable.

245
00:17:12.422 --> 00:17:16.942
So they could test it in one street first, see whether it worked.

246
00:17:16.942 --> 00:17:20.582
It was very low threshold, because it just cost maybe a few thousand

247
00:17:20.582 --> 00:17:23.582
dollars. And then when they noticed that it worked,

248
00:17:23.582 --> 00:17:25.452
they expanded it to other streets.

249
00:17:26.622 --> 00:17:31.902
And this is how they are now changing the environment in

250
00:17:31.902 --> 00:17:35.692
Houston for a different future, one with a lot more rainfall.

251
00:17:36.422 --> 00:17:38.052
And that is a robust solution,

252
00:17:39.702 --> 00:17:42.702
because you can scale it up, you can scale it down.

253
00:17:42.702 --> 00:17:45.852
If it doesn't work, you reject it and look for something different.

254
00:17:46.462 --> 00:17:49.612
It is also very easy to implement.

255
00:17:50.582 --> 00:17:54.972
So that is the kind of solutions that work in stress situations.

256
00:17:57.302 --> 00:18:02.942
Thank you, Mr. Vatkov. Does this remind you of any solution in your

257
00:18:02.942 --> 00:18:06.942
domain that has been deployed in the Bulgarian environment as well that

258
00:18:06.942 --> 00:18:11.542
could be transposed as a good example, maybe related to

259
00:18:11.542 --> 00:18:13.092
cybersecurity as well?

260
00:18:14.342 --> 00:18:19.823
Well, I don't have observations of the Bulgarian and Varna IT sector,

261
00:18:19.823 --> 00:18:26.693
but, you know, the topic of robustness versus resilience,

262
00:18:27.503 --> 00:18:29.893
we need both at the end of the day.

263
00:18:30.543 --> 00:18:35.063
And it is kind of fitting to the topic of security by design

264
00:18:35.063 --> 00:18:41.013
as well. You cannot afford to build truly

265
00:18:41.903 --> 00:18:47.183
robust systems always, because it comes with a price tag,

266
00:18:47.183 --> 00:18:53.103
right? So, yes, you can try to be smart and you can find a

267
00:18:53.103 --> 00:18:56.573
pragmatic solution like the mentioned example.

268
00:18:57.383 --> 00:19:04.183
You can also try to find the critical paths that need to be

269
00:19:04.183 --> 00:19:06.373
robust in your environment.

270
00:19:07.343 --> 00:19:10.423
But at the end of the day, you also need to prepare to be

271
00:19:10.423 --> 00:19:14.423
resilient, because let's be honest,

272
00:19:14.423 --> 00:19:18.423
it is not a question whether an incident is going to happen, if

273
00:19:18.423 --> 00:19:20.093
something bad is going to happen.

274
00:19:21.023 --> 00:19:25.183
But the question is when it will happen, and at the end of the

275
00:19:25.183 --> 00:19:29.983
day, also a very important question that an organization needs to

276
00:19:29.983 --> 00:19:36.893
answer is, how fast can we identify such an incident?

277
00:19:37.583 --> 00:19:43.383
Are we prepared to mitigate it, to contain it, and to recover out of

278
00:19:43.383 --> 00:19:49.653
it? And these are also aspects that belong to the broader

279
00:19:50.463 --> 00:19:53.573
design of robustness, I would say.

280
00:19:55.463 --> 00:20:01.973
Thank you. I would like us to talk a bit about the

281
00:20:02.383 --> 00:20:04.813
management part of

282
00:20:06.103 --> 00:20:10.493
advancing innovation and building successful

283
00:20:11.983 --> 00:20:13.213
product smart solutions.

284
00:20:13.823 --> 00:20:20.423
Yesterday in one of the opening policy panels, one

285
00:20:20.423 --> 00:20:24.785
of the speakers mentioned that Bulgarian companies need to

286
00:20:24.785 --> 00:20:28.505
improve their management practices significantly to

287
00:20:28.505 --> 00:20:34.785
grow and scale. So this is kind of a follow-up question

288
00:20:34.785 --> 00:20:39.745
for both of you when it comes to management, could be in the

289
00:20:39.745 --> 00:20:41.975
private sector but in the public sector as well.

290
00:20:42.505 --> 00:20:46.535
What do we need to change to deploy technology

291
00:20:47.865 --> 00:20:48.935
more competitively?

292
00:20:50.105 --> 00:20:56.705
And Mr. Vatkov, I would also like if you can maybe comment

293
00:20:56.705 --> 00:21:00.425
if in your opinion management is also an issue

294
00:21:00.425 --> 00:21:03.295
in the Bulgarian private sector and

295
00:21:05.465 --> 00:21:09.655
how we can approach it if it's an issue.

296
00:21:11.185 --> 00:21:16.465
Well, I can put it from my perspective, and I'm dealing

297
00:21:16.465 --> 00:21:18.335
with cybersecurity topics mainly.

298
00:21:19.105 --> 00:21:25.705
So, looking at the regulations that are

299
00:21:25.705 --> 00:21:29.615
popping up in the last years

300
00:21:30.665 --> 00:21:35.945
that are stressing on having additional governance

301
00:21:35.945 --> 00:21:41.265
into the organizations that we are associated

302
00:21:41.265 --> 00:21:47.945
with, and making governance a managerial topic,

303
00:21:47.945 --> 00:21:53.745
but also pushing liability topics, personal liability topics, to

304
00:21:53.745 --> 00:21:54.985
the management.

305
00:21:54.985 --> 00:21:57.495
So, I think a significant

306
00:21:58.865 --> 00:22:02.665
change that needs to happen first is to realize and to be aware

307
00:22:02.665 --> 00:22:03.615
of those changes.

308
00:22:04.785 --> 00:22:07.985
This is the first point.

309
00:22:07.985 --> 00:22:10.815
And then, of course,

310
00:22:12.025 --> 00:22:17.505
to start to ask yourself how does such regulation apply to

311
00:22:17.505 --> 00:22:22.505
our organizational context, or how such changes in the

312
00:22:22.505 --> 00:22:26.176
environment apply to the organizational context.

313
00:22:26.826 --> 00:22:31.896
I don't think that we have sufficient efficiency.

314
00:22:33.266 --> 00:22:36.936
Business is there to focus on business.

315
00:22:38.346 --> 00:22:40.536
I think, however,

316
00:22:41.506 --> 00:22:45.216
we need to start to balance things better,

317
00:22:46.706 --> 00:22:50.776
to not aim for point

318
00:22:51.826 --> 00:22:55.216
solutions, but to think about

319
00:22:56.466 --> 00:23:02.386
strategy, because strategy is determining the technology and

320
00:23:02.386 --> 00:23:09.066
not vice versa. I mean, technology is shaping our context

321
00:23:09.066 --> 00:23:10.856
in which we have our opportunities.

322
00:23:12.026 --> 00:23:15.706
But at the end of the day, if an organization doesn't have a

323
00:23:15.706 --> 00:23:22.586
clear strategy, more probably we are going to land

324
00:23:22.586 --> 00:23:25.936
in inefficient managerial decisions.

325
00:23:27.186 --> 00:23:31.226
Thank you, Dr. Leijten, your take on strategy and management and how

326
00:23:31.226 --> 00:23:37.186
they build innovation. Well, I agree with my

327
00:23:37.186 --> 00:23:40.176
colleague. I think one of the problems,

328
00:23:40.866 --> 00:23:43.306
and this may be a bit controversial for me to say,

329
00:23:43.306 --> 00:23:46.136
is that we should not organize everything in projects.

330
00:23:47.106 --> 00:23:49.986
And it is a bit tricky for me to say that, because I actually teach a

331
00:23:49.986 --> 00:23:52.136
course in project management at TU Delft.

332
00:23:52.706 --> 00:23:56.096
But the problem with a project is it's a one-off thing.

333
00:23:56.906 --> 00:24:01.546
And when it's finished, the one who built the project,

334
00:24:01.546 --> 00:24:04.696
they hand over the key to the owner, and that's it.

335
00:24:05.346 --> 00:24:08.536
And what happens afterwards is none of their business,

336
00:24:08.946 --> 00:24:10.546
and it should be their business.

337
00:24:10.546 --> 00:24:16.826
So instead, you can organize things in more like a

338
00:24:16.826 --> 00:24:21.386
process setting, where the one who builds the system also gets some

339
00:24:21.386 --> 00:24:23.616
responsibility over the functioning of the system.

340
00:24:24.386 --> 00:24:28.827
And that way they have the incentive to build it more robustly

341
00:24:28.827 --> 00:24:34.587
again. But you can also, you know, from a management and

342
00:24:34.587 --> 00:24:38.417
governance point of view, you can manage them that way.

343
00:24:38.908 --> 00:24:43.067
So, the key performance indicators on which you assess them are

344
00:24:43.067 --> 00:24:45.137
not so much that they deliver what they promised,

345
00:24:45.547 --> 00:24:49.707
you know, the physical artifact, but also does it do what it was

346
00:24:49.707 --> 00:24:53.017
supposed to do? So,

347
00:24:54.107 --> 00:24:55.297
is the service available?

348
00:24:56.427 --> 00:24:59.218
If there is a disruption, how quickly is it resolved?

349
00:25:00.307 --> 00:25:04.017
And that way, you give them a different incentive.

350
00:25:05.067 --> 00:25:08.268
And my experience is that they will come up with all kinds of smart

351
00:25:08.268 --> 00:25:11.908
solutions they would never have come up with if it had been a

352
00:25:11.908 --> 00:25:14.537
project and they just had to hand over the key.

353
00:25:15.587 --> 00:25:19.908
So, you shift responsibility, and I can give you examples of this.

354
00:25:19.908 --> 00:25:23.668
I don't know, considering the cybersecurity and AI topic

355
00:25:23.668 --> 00:25:26.668
we're going to talk about, it may not be of much importance to the audience,

356
00:25:26.668 --> 00:25:32.187
but this is, for example, how the maintenance of the railway network

357
00:25:32.187 --> 00:25:34.897
in the Netherlands has been organized over the past 20 years.

358
00:25:35.307 --> 00:25:37.827
So, previously it was always a one-off project.

359
00:25:37.827 --> 00:25:40.867
You have to replace that switch, you have to paint that bridge or

360
00:25:40.867 --> 00:25:44.377
whatever. The job was done, and then when it was finished,

361
00:25:44.908 --> 00:25:48.268
you know, they handed over the key, so to say, and that was it.

362
00:25:48.268 --> 00:25:52.107
Whether the switch functioned, whether there were disruptions was of

363
00:25:52.107 --> 00:25:53.218
no importance.

364
00:25:54.268 --> 00:25:58.227
Then they changed into a way of working where a certified

365
00:25:58.227 --> 00:26:01.388
contractor gets responsibility for a part of the railway network for

366
00:26:01.388 --> 00:26:06.177
5 years. And they will be assessed on

367
00:26:07.148 --> 00:26:09.377
what percentage of the time the railway was available.

368
00:26:10.227 --> 00:26:12.388
How long did disruptions take?

369
00:26:12.388 --> 00:26:14.297
So, how quickly did you resolve them?

370
00:26:14.707 --> 00:26:21.668
So, how much time does it take for maintenance

371
00:26:21.668 --> 00:26:25.028
work to take place? So, there was an incentive to work during the

372
00:26:25.028 --> 00:26:27.148
nights and the weekends rather than, you know,

373
00:26:27.148 --> 00:26:32.749
during busy times. The contractors came with solutions

374
00:26:32.749 --> 00:26:36.579
like tougher steel so that there is less wear.

375
00:26:37.309 --> 00:26:41.509
They came up with additional work sheds so they could actually

376
00:26:41.509 --> 00:26:44.469
access the railway a lot quicker than from one place.

377
00:26:44.469 --> 00:26:47.069
So all kinds of things that have never happened before because

378
00:26:47.069 --> 00:26:48.819
everything had been organized in projects.

379
00:26:49.749 --> 00:26:51.059
So there was really

380
00:26:52.069 --> 00:26:55.949
an optimal way of working that had been developed because of this

381
00:26:55.949 --> 00:26:59.509
different way of organizing the governance of the

382
00:26:59.509 --> 00:27:02.709
infrastructure. Well, that's really insightful.

383
00:27:02.709 --> 00:27:08.029
I think it's also kind of a great recommendation in our use

384
00:27:08.029 --> 00:27:11.859
case in Bulgaria and in many, many different

385
00:27:12.909 --> 00:27:19.469
sectors. I would like to ask the public if you have any

386
00:27:19.469 --> 00:27:23.219
questions that you would like to ask our speakers.

387
00:27:24.909 --> 00:27:31.669
Yes. Thank you so much for

388
00:27:31.669 --> 00:27:33.269
the very informative discussion.

389
00:27:33.269 --> 00:27:38.789
It's really great, and I just want to pinpoint

390
00:27:38.789 --> 00:27:42.429
this very last point that was actually mentioned, because I think it's

391
00:27:42.429 --> 00:27:46.109
a really great example that you've described there with regard to

392
00:27:46.109 --> 00:27:50.629
the shift from project-based to more responsibility-based

393
00:27:50.629 --> 00:27:53.859
management, so to speak, if I can call it like that.

394
00:27:54.309 --> 00:27:58.949
And I'm also picking up on a point that Georgi mentioned

395
00:27:58.949 --> 00:28:03.029
that may be something that we should sort of implement

396
00:28:03.029 --> 00:28:04.949
here in terms of a recommendation.

397
00:28:04.949 --> 00:28:08.949
So I would probe you a little bit, both of you actually, because we

398
00:28:08.949 --> 00:28:13.789
heard yesterday a lot that Varna has a lot of potential and there

399
00:28:13.789 --> 00:28:20.109
is a lot of the preconditions for having the smart

400
00:28:20.109 --> 00:28:23.669
solutions, for having the innovation, and for being an innovative

401
00:28:23.669 --> 00:28:28.869
hub are here. So if we are to implement that kind of a framework

402
00:28:28.869 --> 00:28:34.510
from essentially moving away from just project-based to more

403
00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:39.470
responsibility-based management on, let's say, an ecosystem level here

404
00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:46.300
regionally. What this may look like and what would it entail and

405
00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:53.390
how fast do you think that could happen,

406
00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:57.910
if you could think in terms of just stages or like a possible

407
00:28:57.910 --> 00:29:00.470
time frame. I think that would be really helpful

408
00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:01.980
and that would be really great.

409
00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:04.990
Thank you so much. I'm really sorry for putting you on the spot with

410
00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:06.790
that. I know that it's a very tricky question,

411
00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:09.270
but very, very curious about your thoughts.

412
00:29:09.270 --> 00:29:12.900
Thank you. Shall I start?

413
00:29:14.630 --> 00:29:19.070
So, I'm not entirely sure about the point where you start from,

414
00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:21.660
so that's difficult for me to say how long it would take.

415
00:29:22.150 --> 00:29:26.830
What it would look like probably is like the development of a

416
00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:30.980
community again. A versatile community.

417
00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:35.910
Because new ideas typically emerge in the more versatile

418
00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:40.460
communities, not a community where everyone thinks alike.

419
00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:43.500
So, that would be one thing.

420
00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:46.940
And another thing is

421
00:29:48.830 --> 00:29:55.550
So, well, maybe let me let you in on

422
00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:59.110
one of the hiccups that has been experienced in the Netherlands

423
00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:00.300
with this way of working.

424
00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:05.950
In all honesty, and I didn't make that part of my previous answer,

425
00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:09.550
it should have been perhaps, is that in the Netherlands we're moving

426
00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:12.060
away from this system again, unfortunately.

427
00:30:13.230 --> 00:30:16.430
So, the manager of the railways in the Netherlands,

428
00:30:16.430 --> 00:30:18.540
they are reconsidering this way of working.

429
00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:22.470
And the reason they have this is they no

430
00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:24.380
longer have control over the data.

431
00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:30.790
So, they have limited information about the state of the railways,

432
00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:32.540
which is something that makes them uncomfortable.

433
00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:35.510
Which is very imaginable, of course.

434
00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:38.102
So, they are responsible for the state of railways.

435
00:30:39.032 --> 00:30:42.952
So, if the contractors are the only ones who would have this

436
00:30:42.952 --> 00:30:45.582
information, they feel not in control.

437
00:30:46.992 --> 00:30:50.712
Now, my reply to that, you know, when I heard about this and

438
00:30:50.712 --> 00:30:53.072
spoke with people who work for this organization,

439
00:30:53.072 --> 00:30:55.542
is said, "Well, then you didn't organize it the right way."

440
00:30:56.032 --> 00:30:59.552
So, why did you leave all the responsibility for data and

441
00:30:59.552 --> 00:31:01.152
information with the contractors?

442
00:31:01.152 --> 00:31:02.742
You could have set this up differently.

443
00:31:03.592 --> 00:31:06.752
Probably they would have, with hindsight knowledge,

444
00:31:06.752 --> 00:31:08.862
if they knew this 20 years before,

445
00:31:09.592 --> 00:31:11.262
they would have done it that way.

446
00:31:12.392 --> 00:31:14.351
So, now you have to repair this.

447
00:31:14.351 --> 00:31:17.072
And now, the good thing for you is you do not have to do that

448
00:31:17.072 --> 00:31:20.472
because you can organize it the right way straight from the

449
00:31:20.472 --> 00:31:25.992
beginning. So, you need a sort of collaboration where you

450
00:31:25.992 --> 00:31:27.912
make agreements on these things.

451
00:31:27.912 --> 00:31:30.312
Like, what is the shared knowledge?

452
00:31:30.312 --> 00:31:33.182
The knowledge that we share with each other.

453
00:31:33.632 --> 00:31:37.872
A lot of this knowledge may have strategic importance to

454
00:31:37.872 --> 00:31:41.872
the individual stakeholders, so they're not naturally willing to

455
00:31:41.872 --> 00:31:45.152
share it. But maybe if you come to an agreement,

456
00:31:45.152 --> 00:31:49.032
on the basis of reciprocity or whatever the process

457
00:31:49.032 --> 00:31:53.352
would be, you could come up with a smart solution for this.

458
00:31:53.352 --> 00:31:56.862
And you would not have to move back to the old-fashioned days.

459
00:32:00.192 --> 00:32:04.502
Well, let me answer the question as an architect.

460
00:32:07.832 --> 00:32:11.912
What it takes? Well, it needs a common vision.

461
00:32:11.912 --> 00:32:13.742
I believe this is the first point.

462
00:32:14.512 --> 00:32:17.392
And common vision not only from the industry,

463
00:32:17.392 --> 00:32:21.862
but also from the public bodies, from the local administration,

464
00:32:23.112 --> 00:32:26.862
the support of the Bulgarian administration,

465
00:32:27.632 --> 00:32:31.062
the support of the wider European community,

466
00:32:31.632 --> 00:32:34.662
aligned on this common vision at the end of the day.

467
00:32:35.312 --> 00:32:38.302
This is where we start.

468
00:32:38.792 --> 00:32:44.233
And then the second thing is to develop a common strategy to come closer

469
00:32:44.233 --> 00:32:47.273
to this vision.

470
00:32:47.273 --> 00:32:51.033
What does it take? What is the aim at the end of the day?

471
00:32:51.033 --> 00:32:56.593
Why do we want to be there, where we envision to be, in a

472
00:32:56.593 --> 00:33:00.553
certain period of time? I mean, the time question is always kind

473
00:33:00.553 --> 00:33:05.113
of difficult to answer in these situations, because

474
00:33:05.113 --> 00:33:08.263
usually you can answer this question only afterwards.

475
00:33:08.953 --> 00:33:12.033
But you can have an aim, and you can develop this, and you can align

476
00:33:12.033 --> 00:33:18.143
on common goals, and, well, I believe

477
00:33:19.113 --> 00:33:20.013
in sustainability.

478
00:33:21.673 --> 00:33:25.463
That's why I would not recommend

479
00:33:26.593 --> 00:33:32.543
you to focus on the hypes but to think of what the advantages

480
00:33:33.593 --> 00:33:38.063
of the region are. I mean, there were so many mentioned yesterday.

481
00:33:38.753 --> 00:33:43.063
Four universities, I believe, the port, the airport,

482
00:33:43.593 --> 00:33:45.663
the infrastructure is getting better.

483
00:33:46.273 --> 00:33:49.193
Of course, not everything is perfect,

484
00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:54.793
but I believe these are just kind of dots that are not yet

485
00:33:54.793 --> 00:33:58.223
probably identified or not connected into this common vision.

486
00:33:58.753 --> 00:34:03.153
Yeah. So, this is from my perspective the way we

487
00:34:03.153 --> 00:34:04.583
should aim for this.

488
00:34:08.353 --> 00:34:09.753
Thank you for those answers.

489
00:34:09.753 --> 00:34:13.793
If we don't have any more questions, we're just right on time,

490
00:34:13.793 --> 00:34:15.263
so we can end this discussion.

491
00:34:15.873 --> 00:34:19.593
I think overall we covered the topic really well.

492
00:34:19.593 --> 00:34:23.713
We talked about the technology itself, the different approaches

493
00:34:23.713 --> 00:34:28.393
on building the systems, but as well as the management and

494
00:34:28.393 --> 00:34:33.793
strategy that goes into advancing such innovation for critical

495
00:34:33.793 --> 00:34:39.433
infrastructure protection, which is another crucial angle, as

496
00:34:39.433 --> 00:34:45.354
well as this difference of how we should move

497
00:34:45.354 --> 00:34:49.864
beyond projects and have a more systematic approach.

498
00:34:50.274 --> 00:34:53.264
So, please have a round of applause for our speaker.
